{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788975786,"nonce":"P9R3naS2RspTCVlZRVmDFCZNL8E=","title":"Strip-mine loops so the fiber poll leaves the innermost loop","message":"A poll is emitted at every loop header, including innermost ones. In nbody's\n`advance` the inner loop carries the epoch load, compare and branch on every\niteration, plus the array-base reload the poll's memory clobber prevents LICM\nfrom hoisting.\n\nRemoving polls entirely is worth 4.6% on nbody, 9.9% on method_call and 7.3% on\nclosure_call, but is not sound -- without them a compiled loop never reaches a\nsafepoint, so the collector cannot stop the world and a fiber never yields.\nDeclaring the helper `memory(inaccessiblemem: readwrite)` recovers only a\nminority of that (and none of it on closure_call), and is unsound anyway because\nthe far side of a yield runs Haxe code against the same heap.\n\nStrip-mining is the fix HotSpot uses: turn the loop into an outer trip counter\nover blocks of N iterations with the poll on the outer header, leaving the inner\nloop free of the call, the branch and the barrier together while keeping\nsafepoint latency bounded.","files":null}]}